    "documentation": {
      "format": "markdown",
      "content": "**Scientific study:** [dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength on GitHub](https://github.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength)  \n**Dataset source:** [Concrete Compressive Strength — UCI Machine Learning Repository](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/165/concrete+compressive+strength)\n\nThis dataset presents a **continuous-regression** problem based on eight numerical mixture and curing-age measurements. The objective is to estimate **Concrete compressive strength** as a continuous numerical value on its original MPa scale.\n\nThe analysis is predictive rather than causal. Relationships between ingredients, aggregate quantities, water, curing age, and measured strength describe statistical structure in the observed data; they do not establish the causal effect of changing an individual input.\n\n## Dataset overview\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---:|\n| Observations | 1,030 |\n| Source columns | 9 |\n| Predictive features | 8 numerical features |\n| Target | `Concrete compressive strength` |\n| Target unit | MPa |\n| Problem type | Continuous regression |\n| Selected model | HistGradientBoostingRegressor |\n| Feature policy | `all_features` |\n| Primary model-selection metric | MAE |\n| Validation MAE | **2.7417 MPa** |\n| Final-test MAE | **2.5822 MPa** |\n| Final-test RMSE | 4.2104 MPa |\n| Final-test R² | 0.9387 |\n| Final-test MedAE | 1.6363 MPa |\n\nEach row represents one concrete formulation and its measured compressive strength.\n\nThe eight predictive features are:\n\n```text\nCement\nBlast Furnace Slag\nFly Ash\nWater\nSuperplasticizer\nCoarse Aggregate\nFine Aggregate\nAge\n```\n\nThe continuous target is:\n\n```text\nConcrete compressive strength\n```\n\nNo classification labels, positive-class semantics, thresholds, or argmax decisions are involved.\n\n## Analytical question\n\nThe analysis asks whether the eight available numerical formulation and age variables contain sufficient predictive signal to estimate concrete compressive strength for previously unseen observations.\n\nThe fitted regressor returns one continuous prediction in MPa.\n\nThis prediction is conditional on the supplied measurements and on patterns learned from the study sample. It is not a causal estimate of how much strength would change if one ingredient or curing condition were altered.\n\n## Feature and target contract\n\n| Role | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| Numerical predictors | `Cement`, `Blast Furnace Slag`, `Fly Ash`, `Water`, `Superplasticizer`, `Coarse Aggregate`, `Fine Aggregate`, `Age` |\n| Target | `Concrete compressive strength` |\n| Target semantics | Continuous / quantitative |\n| Output unit | MPa |\n| Identifier | None provided by source |\n| Categorical predictors | None |\n\nThe target is never admitted as a predictor.\n\nBecause no observation identifier is provided, exact equality between rows or feature profiles cannot establish that records represent the same physical concrete observation.\n\n## Data quality and preparation\n\nThe source contains 1,030 rows and nine columns, and all target values are present and finite.\n\nA notable metadata discrepancy concerns `Blast Furnace Slag`: the source metadata declares an integer type, while the released table contains valid decimal measurements.\n\nThe study preserves the released measurements exactly:\n\n- 298 rows, or 28.932%, contain non-integer slag values;\n- no rounding or truncation is applied;\n- no rows are removed because of the metadata mismatch;\n- no generic imputation is introduced.\n\nRepeated observations are also preserved:\n\n| Repetition evidence | Result |\n|---|---:|\n| Exact-row groups | 11 |\n| Rows in exact-row groups | 36 |\n| Repeated feature-profile groups | 19 |\n| Rows in repeated feature-profile groups | 57 |\n| Repeated-profile groups with shared target | 10 groups / 33 rows |\n| Repeated-profile groups with target disagreement | 9 groups / 24 rows |\n\nEquality alone is not treated as proof of duplicate identity.\n\nNo generic outlier deletion, clipping, winsorization, target binning, or target transformation is applied. All eight validated predictors are retained.\n\n## Target and exploratory findings\n\n### Continuous target distribution\n\nThe target is evaluated on its original MPa scale.\n\n![Concrete compressive strength target distribution](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength/main/docs/images/target_distribution.png)\n\nObserved extremes and IQR-based flags are treated as descriptive diagnostics rather than automatic deletion rules.\n\n### Several predictors show measurable association with strength\n\n![Feature-to-target association ranking](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength/main/docs/images/feature_target_association_ranking.png)\n\nThe exploratory analysis identifies multiple predictors with meaningful univariate association to the continuous target.\n\nThese relationships do not establish independent causal importance. Their predictive value must be assessed jointly under leakage-safe validation.\n\n### The numerical feature space contains dependence\n\n![Numerical feature correlation heatmap](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength/main/docs/images/numerical_feature_correlation_heatmap.png)\n\nCorrelation structure is preserved as exploratory evidence rather than converted into automatic feature deletion.\n\n### Nonlinear and interaction structure is plausible\n\n![Regression nonlinearity signals](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength/main/docs/images/regression_nonlinearity_signals.png)\n\n![Regression interaction signals](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength/main/docs/images/regression_interaction_signals.png)\n\nThe exploratory analysis records three nonlinearity signals and four interaction signals. This motivates direct comparison between an additive linear model and more flexible tree-based regression families.\n\nThe diagnostics themselves are not model-performance evidence.\n\n## Preparation and evaluation split\n\nThe study uses a reproducible shuffled, non-stratified regression split:\n\n| Partition | Rows | Share | Purpose |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| Train | 721 | 70% | Cross-validation and hyperparameter search |\n| Validation | 154 | 15% | Frozen candidate comparison and selection |\n| Final test | 155 | 15% | One-time independent holdout |\n\nThe split uses random seed `42`, with second-stage seed `43`.\n\nNo target bins are created for stratification.\n\nThe final-test partition remains sealed through model-family comparison, preprocessing choice, hyperparameter search, and validation selection.\n\nLearned numerical scaling is model-dependent and fitted only on training data. Ridge uses scaling inside its pipeline; tree-based candidates operate on the original numerical representation.\n\n## Evaluation protocol\n\n**Mean Absolute Error (MAE)** is the primary model-selection metric and is expressed in MPa.\n\nComplementary metrics are:\n\n- RMSE;\n- R²;\n- Median Absolute Error.\n\nHyperparameter selection uses training-only five-fold cross-validation:\n\n```text\nKFold\nn_splits = 5\nshuffle = True\nrandom_state = 42\n```\n\nThe practical-tie tolerance is:\n\n```text\n0.10 MPa\n```\n\nIf multiple candidates fall within that tolerance on validation MAE, the predefined tie-break order is:\n\n1. lower RMSE;\n2. lower MedAE;\n3. lower CV-MAE standard deviation;\n4. higher R²;\n5. stable model identity.\n\nThe final-test partition never participates in model selection.\n\n## Model selection\n\nThe study compares a median baseline with Ridge, Decision Tree, Random Forest, and HistGradientBoosting regression.\n\n### Candidate comparison\n\nEach trainable family is first tuned using train-only cross-validation. Its best family configuration is then frozen and evaluated once on the validation partition.\n\n| Model | MAE ↓ | RMSE ↓ | R² ↑ | MedAE ↓ | Result |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| HistGradientBoostingRegressor | **2.7417** | **4.0870** | **0.9336** | **1.8033** | **Selected** |\n| RandomForestRegressor | 3.7689 | 5.4146 | 0.8835 | 2.5385 | Candidate |\n| DecisionTreeRegressor | 4.2860 | 6.5247 | 0.8309 | 2.9000 | Candidate |\n| Ridge | 8.5944 | 10.7533 | 0.5406 | 7.1671 | Candidate |\n| Dummy median | 12.8145 | 15.8699 | -0.0005 | 11.2600 | Baseline |\n\nEvery trainable candidate improves on the median baseline.\n\nHistGradientBoostingRegressor reduces validation MAE by approximately **10.0728 MPa**, or **78.61%**, relative to the baseline.\n\nIts validation MAE is also more than 0.10 MPa below the next-best candidate, so the selection does not require a practical-tie resolution.\n\n### Candidate search configuration and hyperparameters\n\nThe four trainable families evaluate 52 hyperparameter configurations in total.\n\n| Model | Search policy | Evaluated configurations | Hyperparameters evaluated / fixed constructor |\n|---|---|---:|---|\n| Ridge | GridSearchCV | 4 | `alpha={0.1,1.0,10.0,100.0}`; numerical scaling fitted inside each training fold |\n| Decision Tree | GridSearchCV | 12 | `max_depth={3,5,8,None}`; `min_samples_leaf={1,5,10}`; fixed `random_state=42` |\n| Random Forest | GridSearchCV | 12 | `max_depth={None,12}`; `min_samples_leaf={1,3,5}`; `max_features={1.0,sqrt}`; fixed `n_estimators=300`, `random_state=42`, `n_jobs=1` |\n| HistGradientBoosting | GridSearchCV | 24 | `learning_rate={0.05,0.10}`; `max_leaf_nodes={15,31}`; `min_samples_leaf={10,20,40}`; `l2_regularization={0.0,1.0}`; fixed `max_iter=300`, `random_state=42` |\n| Dummy median | No search | 1 | `strategy=median` |\n\nThe complete search spaces above are part of the reproducible study contract. Exact frozen best-parameter combinations for non-selected families are not invented when the corresponding runtime artifacts are not versioned.\n\n### Selected configuration\n\nThe selected model is HistGradientBoostingRegressor using all eight features.\n\n| Hyperparameter | Selected value |\n|---|---:|\n| `learning_rate` | 0.1 |\n| `max_leaf_nodes` | 15 |\n| `min_samples_leaf` | 10 |\n| `l2_regularization` | 1.0 |\n| `max_iter` | 300 |\n| `random_state` | 42 |\n\nNo numerical scaling is applied to the selected model.\n\n## Final holdout evaluation\n\nAfter every selection decision is frozen, the selected model is trained once on train plus validation:\n\n```text\n875 observations\n```\n\nIt is then evaluated once on the sealed 155-row final-test partition.\n\n| Metric | Validation | Final test | Test − validation |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| **MAE (MPa)** | 2.7417 | **2.5822** | -0.1595 |\n| RMSE (MPa) | 4.0870 | **4.2104** | +0.1234 |\n| R² | 0.9336 | **0.9387** | +0.0050 |\n| MedAE (MPa) | 1.8033 | **1.6363** | -0.1669 |\n\nThe final holdout remains broadly consistent with validation: MAE and MedAE improve slightly, R² increases slightly, and RMSE increases modestly.\n\n### Error diagnostics\n\n| Diagnostic | Final-test value |\n|---|---:|\n| Residual mean | 0.7577 MPa |\n| Residual standard deviation | 4.1551 MPa |\n| Maximum absolute error | 26.6252 MPa |\n| Absolute error p50 | 1.6363 MPa |\n| Absolute error p90 | 6.4306 MPa |\n| Absolute error p95 | 7.7465 MPa |\n\nThese values describe the holdout error distribution and are not used to retune the model.\n\n## Understanding the evaluation metrics\n\n### MAE — 2.5822 MPa\n\nMAE is the average absolute difference between prediction and observed compressive strength.\n\nWithin the final-test sample, predictions differ from the measured target by about `2.58 MPa` on average.\n\n**Lower values are better.**\n\n### RMSE — 4.2104 MPa\n\nRMSE gives greater weight to larger errors than MAE.\n\nIts value above MAE indicates that a smaller number of larger misses contribute materially to the overall error profile.\n\n**Lower values are better.**\n\n### R² — 0.9387\n\nR² describes predictive fit relative to a simple variance-based reference.\n\nThe value indicates strong explanatory predictive fit within this frozen random-snapshot holdout, but it does not express error magnitude in MPa.\n\n**Higher values are better.**\n\n### MedAE — 1.6363 MPa\n\nMedian Absolute Error is the median magnitude of prediction error.\n\nHalf of the final-test absolute errors are at or below approximately `1.64 MPa`.\n\n**Lower values are better.**\n\n## Prediction and result interpretation\n\nThe fitted model accepts the same eight numerical measurements used during training:\n\n```text\nCement\nBlast Furnace Slag\nFly Ash\nWater\nSuperplasticizer\nCoarse Aggregate\nFine Aggregate\nAge\n```\n\nIt returns one continuous numerical estimate:\n\n```text\nConcrete compressive strength\n```\n\nin:\n\n```text\nMPa\n```\n\nThe input contract rejects missing or non-finite required values.\n\nIllustrative inference examples produce:\n\n| Example | Predicted strength |\n|---|---:|\n| Early-age mix | 29.994813 MPa |\n| Standard mix | 46.098200 MPa |\n| Slag/fly-ash mix | 53.969705 MPa |\n| High-cement mix | 67.761977 MPa |\n\nThese manually written examples have no observed target and are not additional performance measurements.\n\nA prediction is an estimate conditional on the supplied feature values. It is not:\n\n- a guaranteed future laboratory measurement;\n- a causal estimate of changing one ingredient;\n- a substitute for physical testing;\n- evidence of validity under a different material, laboratory, standard, or data-generating process.\n\n## What this study demonstrates\n\nThe dataset provides a compact continuous-regression case with:\n\n- eight numerical predictors;\n- a continuous physical target on an interpretable unit scale;\n- source-metadata quality review;\n- repeated-row and repeated-profile ambiguity without identifiers;\n- preservation rather than generic outlier cleaning;\n- leakage-aware train-validation-test separation;\n- train-only preprocessing and hyperparameter search;\n- a median regression baseline;\n- comparison of linear, single-tree, bagging, and boosting families;\n- explicit search spaces for every candidate family;\n- validation-based model selection;\n- a sealed one-time final holdout;\n- aggregate residual diagnostics;\n- trusted independent continuous inference.\n\nThe study also shows why regression evaluation should not be reduced to a single score. MAE provides an interpretable average error in MPa, RMSE highlights larger misses, R² describes overall predictive fit, and MedAE characterizes the typical error with greater robustness to extremes.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The evaluation is based on a shuffled random snapshot rather than temporal, prospective, or external validation.\n- External representativeness is not established.\n- The source provides no observation identifier.\n- Repeated rows or feature profiles therefore cannot be treated as confirmed duplicate physical observations.\n- The source metadata type for `Blast Furnace Slag` conflicts with valid released decimal measurements.\n- IQR and target-extreme flags are descriptive rather than proof of erroneous observations.\n- The final-test partition contains only 155 observations.\n- Individual prediction uncertainty intervals are not produced.\n- Measurement error in the source variables is not modeled.\n- Performance under different laboratories, materials, standards, environmental conditions, or populations is not established.\n- No causal effect of altering mixture components or curing age is estimated.\n- Operational feature availability and measurement stability are unconfirmed.\n- No production monitoring, drift, or retraining policy is evaluated.\n- Operational suitability is not established.\n\n## Responsible interpretation\n\nThe evidence supports a clear predictive conclusion:\n\n> Within this dataset and frozen shuffled evaluation protocol, the available mixture and curing-age measurements contain strong predictive information for concrete compressive strength. HistGradientBoostingRegressor achieves a final-test MAE of approximately **2.58 MPa**, RMSE of **4.21 MPa**, R² of **0.9387**, and MedAE of approximately **1.64 MPa**.\n\nThe comparison between Ridge and the flexible tree-based candidates also supports the exploratory hypothesis that nonlinear and interaction-aware models better represent the predictive structure present in this dataset.\n\nThe results demonstrate effective prediction within the observed study design. They do not establish causal relationships, prospective performance, external generalization, or operational validity.\n\nUse beyond this analytical setting would require representative external validation, verification of the measurement process, uncertainty assessment, distribution-shift evaluation, monitoring policies, and application-specific error tolerances.\n"
    }
  },
  "source_draft_schema_version": "1.0.0"
}